WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:05.538 line:0%
Today we're on a glacier
in Iceland.

00:00:05.571 --> 00:00:07.774 line:0%
- ( grunts )
- ( laughs )

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:10.076 line:0%
Dan:
<i> This is the Strokkur Geyser.</i>

00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:12.178 align:start position:0%
It goes off every
five to ten minutes.

00:00:12.211 --> 00:00:14.080
Oh! That was a good one.

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:15.481
Dan:<i> Oh, that's big there,
 look at that.</i>

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:17.383
Gav:<i> That is massive.</i>

00:00:17.416 --> 00:00:20.653
Now I've got something
different in mind.

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:22.355 align:start position:0%
Of all the Phantoms
we've ever used,

00:00:22.388 --> 00:00:25.191
this is the biggest.

00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:29.062
Dan:<i> I'm nervous
 for his drone and the camera.</i>

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.263
Might get hit.

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:32.465
<i> Holy crap.</i>

00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:34.534 align:start size:53% position:47%
Oh! Jesus Christ!

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:35.769
( chuckles )

00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:41.441 size:84% position:42% line:0%
Well, looking back on that,

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:43.543 size:78% position:39% line:0%
I think that was the most
challenging conditions

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.244 align:start position:0%
I've ever shot in.

00:00:45.277 --> 00:00:46.712 size:84% position:58%
And it smelled really eggy.

00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:48.214 align:start position:0%
Smells a bit like eggs.

00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:49.348 align:start size:59% position:41%
Sorry, that was me,
that one.

00:00:49.381 --> 00:00:50.817 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Oh, was that you?
- Yeah.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.351
By far the sketchiest position

00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:54.153
that Phantom camera
has ever been in

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:56.756 align:start position:0%
is hovering above
a hole in the Earth

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:58.424 align:start position:0%
and then getting
battered with water.

00:00:58.457 --> 00:00:59.859
Dan: Oh!

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:02.395 align:start size:41% position:59%
Jesus Christ!

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:04.397
Holy crap.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.466
The amount of stress
running through my body

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:08.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
- when I was watching that.
- Oh, God.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.236 align:start size:69% position:31%
It's quite cool that
we had the opportunity
to have the camera

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:11.737 align:start size:66% position:34%
flying above
what we were filming.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.406 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, it's never
  been that high, ever.
- No.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:14.874
I also thought it would be
very cool if we brought along

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:17.143 align:start position:0%
a thermal camera to see
the difference in temperature

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:19.779 align:start position:0%
between us and the water,
so let's take a look at that.

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:21.681
Would you like to tell us
about this camera?

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:23.816 align:start position:0% line:0%
Yeah, this is a FLIR
thermal imaging camera.

00:01:23.849 --> 00:01:25.451 line:0%
This is actually a cold camera

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:27.220 size:84% position:42% line:0%
and what is basically does,
it measures temperatures.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.489
Dan:<i> This is like
 80 to 100 degrees.</i>

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.824
Rudd:<i> Yeah,
 basically every pixel
 is a temperature measurement,</i>

00:01:31.857 --> 00:01:34.360
<i> so you can be really detailed
 on what you want to measure.</i>

00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:37.630 align:start size:78% position:22%
So should we have it
so that it's further back
and I'm in the frame?

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:39.365 align:start size:91% position:9%
- So I'm just shivering...
- Yeah, let's see
  what that looks like.

00:01:39.398 --> 00:01:41.167 align:start size:78% position:22%
Shivering and then, yeah.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.136 align:start position:0%
Well, I guess I'm not operating,
I can be in there with you.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:44.370 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Oh, yeah, okay. Yeah.
- Hey.

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:46.305
All right, let's do it.

00:01:46.338 --> 00:01:47.840 align:start size:94% position:6%
- It's a bit cold, isn't it?
- It's pretty chilly.

00:01:47.873 --> 00:01:49.709 align:start size:97% position:3%
- What is it, like one degree?
- Something like that.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:52.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
- And it's raining as well.
- Yeah.

00:01:52.444 --> 00:01:54.881 align:start position:0%
Shouldn't be long though
until it goes off.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:56.749 size:88% position:56%
I like this one 'cause it's
only like every five minutes

00:01:56.782 --> 00:01:59.185 align:start size:56% position:44%
this one goes off.

00:01:59.218 --> 00:02:00.820 align:start position:0%
Check this out, ready?

00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:03.523 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Oh!
- There you go.

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.558
All right, how was that?

00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:07.360
<i> ( chuckles )
 Look at our little faces.</i>

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.562
Dan:<i> ( laughs )
 I look like I'm evil.</i>

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:11.631
<i> My eyes are warmer
 than the rest of my face.</i>

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:13.699
Gav:<i> You're leaking heat
 through your eyes and neck.</i>

00:02:13.732 --> 00:02:15.668
<i> Oh, yeah.</i>

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:17.237
- Dan:<i> Oh, that's crazy. </i>
- Gav:<i> Isn't that cool?</i>

00:02:25.844 --> 00:02:27.480
- Yeah, that's wicked.
- Then all the steam.

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:28.848 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That's really cool.
- Yeah.

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:31.384 size:81% position:41%
And this is the software
that the FLIR cameras use.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.586 align:start position:0%
So we can actually
see some values.

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:37.290 align:start position:0%
Firstly, let's just see
how cold my nose was.

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:38.324 align:start position:0%
56...

00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:39.792 align:start size:78% position:22%
What is that guff number?

00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:41.894 align:start position:0%
Fahrenheit, you got to change
it, you got to change it.

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:44.297 align:start position:0%
Let's get on Celsius.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.365 align:start size:91% position:9%
I like how your nose is
so much colder than your face

00:02:46.398 --> 00:02:47.600 align:start size:72% position:28%
'cause it's so far away
from the blood flow.

00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:48.868
Got a 14 degree nose.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:50.436
( Dan laughs )

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:51.637 align:start position:0%
But my eyelids are 23.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.573
All right, let's play on.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.875 align:start size:88% position:13%
And what you got to remember
is it's actually
really cold outside,

00:02:55.908 --> 00:02:58.244 align:start size:75% position:25%
so it's so quick
to turn into cool water.

00:02:58.277 --> 00:02:59.979 align:start position:0%
You see how quickly
it fades off

00:03:00.012 --> 00:03:02.748 align:start position:0%
and becomes your greens
and your blues.

00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:03.783 align:start size:38% position:63%
It's wicked.

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.451 align:start position:0%
Looks like a rainbow
got nuked.

00:03:05.484 --> 00:03:06.886 align:start size:25% position:75%
It does.

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:08.688
Yeah, all this stuff
that's still bubbling

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:09.855
is the hottest.

00:03:09.888 --> 00:03:12.258 align:start position:0%
Yeah, 60.
70 degrees there.

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:14.694
Oh, something I've always
wanted to find out.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.229 align:start position:0%
Yeah?

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.731 align:start position:0%
Gav: Let's have a quick look
at your heart.

00:03:18.764 --> 00:03:20.533 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Ooh.
- Cold as ice.

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:22.335 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Cold as ice, five degrees.
- Yeah.

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.904
With an asterisk
so it's probably like...

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:26.539 align:start position:0%
Can't, it's too cold.

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:27.940 size:84% position:58%
'Cause we filmed it in RAW,

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:29.675 align:start size:75% position:25%
it meant that we can
edit this as we're going

00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:31.410 align:start size:53% position:47%
in this software.

00:03:31.443 --> 00:03:32.945
Well, let's see what
the highest temperature it hits

00:03:32.978 --> 00:03:35.514
through the whole thing.

00:03:35.547 --> 00:03:38.417 align:start size:72% position:28%
Spot in--
87, that's pretty high.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.987 align:start size:50% position:50%
86, 85, 83, 82,
it's going down.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.689 align:start size:59% position:41%
So at the beginning
of the eruption

00:03:43.722 --> 00:03:45.591 size:88% position:44%
was the highest temperature.

00:03:45.624 --> 00:03:46.726 align:start size:56% position:44%
Around here, yeah.

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:47.927 align:start position:0%
87.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.862 align:start size:47% position:53%
87.9, about 88.

00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:52.898
88, and that's probably...
yeah, around there.

00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:54.734 size:84% position:42%
It's the hottest it's been.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.969 size:78% position:61%
So pretty much as soon
as it's burst its bubble,

00:03:57.002 --> 00:03:59.038 align:start position:0%
that's when
it's the highest.

00:03:59.071 --> 00:04:00.606 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Gav: All right.
- Dan: It immediately
  turns cooler.

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:02.742 align:start position:0%
Think we've learned
a lot of good stuff there.

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.410 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I've never actually seen
anything like a geyser
in real life.

00:04:04.443 --> 00:04:05.978 align:start size:59% position:41%
It's kinda surreal.

00:04:06.011 --> 00:04:09.315 size:88% position:56%
Although, I don't actually
know what's happening, so...

00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:11.651 align:start position:0%
I mean, we spent all day there
and it's still kinda confusing
how that worked.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:12.918 align:start size:84% position:16%
- A complete mystery.
- Yeah.

00:04:12.951 --> 00:04:14.787 line:0%
So now we've got
John Cotter with us,

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.323 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
who's a professor of geography
and environmental science,

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:19.625
who can hopefully
take us through
exactly what's happening.

00:04:19.658 --> 00:04:21.961 align:start position:0%
We also have this
lovely experiment.

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:23.329 align:start position:0%
Do you want to run us
through this?

00:04:23.362 --> 00:04:24.997
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
Okay, well, water's gonna be
flowing downhill.

00:04:27.466 --> 00:04:29.835
And as it flows downhill,
it's gonna start seeping
into the ground.

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:31.570
<i> So as it's coming down,</i>

00:04:31.603 --> 00:04:33.673
<i> deep below there has to be
 some kind of a boiler.</i>

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:34.840
<i> And so that water's
 gonna get in there,</i>

00:04:34.873 --> 00:04:36.475
<i> it's gonna start heating up.</i>

00:04:36.508 --> 00:04:38.744
<i> And once that starts
 boiling like that,</i>

00:04:38.777 --> 00:04:40.813
<i> it's gonna start getting
 bigger and bigger and bigger.</i>

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:43.716 align:start position:0%
<i>And as it rises, that heavier,
more dense water</i>

00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:45.851
<i> is just gonna come
 and flow underneath it,</i>

00:04:45.884 --> 00:04:48.354
<i> and that gives it
 a little bit more spring.</i>

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:50.957
<i> And once that spring starts,
 you've got a jet heading
 upstream.</i>

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:56.696
It's amazing that
they're so deep and so narrow.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.631 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Yeah.
- Gav: It's Phantom time.

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:00.499 align:start position:0%
John, do you want to explain how
this experiment works for us?

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:02.101
Okay, let's start with
the hot plate down here,

00:05:02.134 --> 00:05:03.903
this represents the magma

00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:05.604
that's gonna be
heating up the water.

00:05:05.637 --> 00:05:07.840
And these bubbles
are getting bigger and bigger

00:05:07.873 --> 00:05:09.642
and coalescing at the top
of the beaker.

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.077
Once that bubble
gets big enough,

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.846 align:start position:0%
it's gonna have enough momentum
to start climbing.

00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:15.948
So if you're on the surface
looking down on this thing,

00:05:15.981 --> 00:05:18.417
what you're gonna see is
this bubble starting to rise

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.519
that tells you that blast
is right on the way.

00:05:20.552 --> 00:05:22.021
Man: Here we go, back up.

00:05:22.054 --> 00:05:24.590 align:start size:78% position:22%
- And...
- Oh, about to go.

00:05:24.623 --> 00:05:27.059 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Oh!
- John: Spectacular.

00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:28.494 align:start size:78% position:22%
Why did we go to Iceland?

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:35.067 align:start position:0%
- Sounds like you after a curry.
- Yeah, I was gonna say
  the same thing,

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.870 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was gonna make the same joke,
but I thought it'd be
in poor taste.

00:05:37.903 --> 00:05:39.872
Gav: And you see the water
go back down, that was cool.

00:05:39.905 --> 00:05:41.507
So it's gonna do it again
and build up again

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:42.575
for a second eruption.

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:45.378
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:48.547
<i> Oh, yeah.
 It's a very realistic scale.</i>

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.050
Dan:<i> Wait, why am I
 way fatter than you are?</i>

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:52.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Gav:<i> I don't know. </i>
- Dan:<i> I just spotted that.</i>

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:55.688
<i> Someone's made like a-- whoa!</i>

00:05:55.721 --> 00:05:57.790 align:start size:97% position:3%
Gav:<i> I like how oddly similar
 this tiny experiment is.</i>

00:05:57.823 --> 00:05:59.692
Dan:<i> It's exactly the same.</i>

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:01.827
<i> It was the same thing, you get
the bubbles of water coming up,</i>

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.662
<i> building up and then erupting,
 it's exactly the same.</i>

00:06:03.695 --> 00:06:05.798
Gav:<i> That's what's great
 about the universe,</i>

00:06:05.831 --> 00:06:08.534
-<i> it's quite consistent. </i>
- Dan:<i> Yeah, that's true.</i>

00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:10.403
Gav:<i> So, John, is this exactly
 what you expected
 from this experiment?</i>

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:12.104
John:<i> I think it's very good</i>

00:06:12.137 --> 00:06:13.939
<i> except these tourists need
to stand a little further away.</i>

00:06:13.972 --> 00:06:15.074
Gav:
<i> They're too close, oh, yeah.</i>

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:16.509
<i> ( laughter )</i>

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.112
Well, we cleaned the ceiling.

00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:21.981 align:start size:91% position:9%
- With boiling hot water.
- It's spotless now.

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:24.417 line:91%
Yeah, it's great. Thanks very
much, John, for your help.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.619 align:start position:0% line:88%
- Appreciate it.
- I actually really feel like
  I've learned something there.

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:28.154 align:start size:78% position:22%
Glad to have had a chance
to meet you. Take care.

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:29.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Very nice to meet you too.
